rved_research_question: Play-type labeling audit: OTHER/DRIVE foul leakage\n- auto_outcome_status: artifact\n- observed_hypothesis: Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts.\n- observed_result: OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)\n- observed_failure_evidence: OTHER PPP inflated by 36% foul endings; DRIVE 12pp below foul benchmark; initiation_type maps 0.7%.\n- observed_sample_and_power: 50,668 possessions in the initiation_type check\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_method: Per-PT diagnostic of foul-ending share vs PPP; initiation_type mapping.\n- observed_verdict: artifact_found"}], "n_results": 2}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
 "research_concepts": [
  "Opponent next-possession PPP",
  "Play-type effectiveness",
  "Lag effects in basketball sequences",
  "Play labeling (e.g., AVOID labels)"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "Measuring the impact of offensive plays on subsequent defensive performance",
  "Handling sequential dependencies in basketball data",
  "Defining and operationalizing 'AVOID' labels or similar outcome classifications",
  "Controlling for confounding variables in play-by-play analysis"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF07",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "After the opponent runs play type X, the next offensive play type Y scores above Y's unconditional H2 baseline, and every PT has a defensive aftershock. A2 aftershock 8/8 PTs replicate all-games and playoffs (CUT +0.119 n=21,398 / PO +0.131 n=1,145 through TRANSITION +0.135 n=26,262 / +0.126 n=1,529)."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts. OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type."
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
   "what_failed": "Assuming that Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts.",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw) due to OTHER PPP inflated by 36% foul endings; DRIVE 12pp below foul benchmark; initiation_type maps 0.7%."
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "A 'defensive aftershock' phenomenon exists, where the opponent's next possession PPP is generally higher after a team's offensive play (FAM:SF07).",
  "Calculated PPP for certain play types (specifically OTHER and DRIVE) is unreliable due to foul leakage and labeling issues, leading to inflated or deflated efficiency metrics (FAM:SF-CHT-025)."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "Whether the magnitude of the defensive aftershock varies significantly enough by play type to warrant 'AVOID' labels, or what criteria would constitute 'large enough' for such labels.",
  "The definition or operationalization of 'AVOID' labels.",
  "The impact of 'own-sequence lags' (i.e., how a team's preceding offensive plays affect its subsequent offensive plays) on opponent next-possession PPP.",
  "How to correct for the identified play-type labeling issues (foul leakage) when analyzing opponent next-possession PPP, or if these issues impact the opponent's subsequent possession labeling."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The research question implies a differential effect of various offensive play types on the opponent's subsequent offensive PPP, with some plays having a sufficiently negative impact to be flagged for avoidance.",
  "The 'AVOID' label concept requires a clear threshold or comparative analysis that is not specified.",
  "The 'own-sequence lags' part of the question suggests looking at dependencies within a team's offensive plays, which is distinct from the 'defensive aftershock' phenomenon."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The specific criteria or threshold for what constitutes 'large enough' to drive AVOID labels.",
  "The precise definition and methodology for creating 'AVOID' labels.",
  "How to account for the identified play-type labeling issues (FAM:SF-CHT-025) in the context of both 'Play X' and the opponent's next possession.",
  "The specific mechanism by which 'own-sequence lags' might influence opponent next-possession PPP."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence confirmed the existence of a 'defensive aftershock' phenomenon, suggesting the core premise is testable (FAM:SF07). However, it also highlighted critical data quality issues with play-type PPP calculations (FAM:SF-CHT-025), which introduce significant methodological risks and necessitate explicit changes to the research design to account for or mitigate these labeling artifacts. This changed the decision from a straightforward 'proceed' to 'proceed_with_changes' and emphasized the need for careful data handling and sensitivity analysis."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.7,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
    "text": "The reliability of play-type labels, particularly for OTHER and DRIVE, is compromised by foul leakage, which inflates or deflates PPP. This could lead to spurious findings regarding the impact of 'Play X' on opponent PPP if 'Play X' or the opponent's subsequent play is affected by these labeling issues."
   },
   {
    "code": "CONFOUNDING",
    "text": "Many factors beyond 'Play X' could influence the opponent's next possession PPP (e.g., substitution patterns, score differential, time of game, foul situation, opponent's personnel). Without proper controls, attributing changes solely to 'Play X' would be confounded."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "FAM:SF07",
   "FAM:SF-CHT-025"
  ],
  "summary": "Prior work has established the existence of a 'defensive aftershock' where a team's offensive play affects the opponent's subsequent possession PPP. However, it also revealed significant data quality issues with play-type labeling and PPP calculation for specific play types, which could create artifactual results. The specific question of 'AVOID labels' and 'own-sequence lags' remains largely unaddressed."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
   "text": "The play-type labels for 'OTHER' and 'DRIVE' have known issues with foul leakage, inflating or deflating their associated PPP. If 'Play X' falls into these categories, or if the opponent's next possession is frequently one of these types, the measured PPP will be unreliable, leading to artifactual conclusions."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "The opponent's next possession PPP is influenced by numerous factors (e.g., game state, personnel on court, transition opportunities, specific defensive schemes). Failing to control for these could lead to spurious correlations between 'Play X' and subsequent opponent PPP."
  },
  {
   "code": "BASE_RATE",
   "text": "Some play types might occur infrequently, especially when considering specific sequences or player combinations, potentially leading to low sample sizes for certain (Play X, Opponent Next PPP) pairs, reducing statistical power and increasing variance."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "The definition of 'AVOID labels' is not yet specified. If these labels are retrospectively derived from the observed PPP outcomes without clear, independent criteria, it could lead to circular reasoning or overfitting."
  },
  {
   "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
   "text": "If comparing the defensive aftershock across numerous play types and considering various 'own-sequence lags,' there's a risk of multiple comparisons inflating the false positive rate without appropriate correction."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.4,
  "rejected": 0.2,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.2,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The average difference in opponent next-possession PPP following specific offensive play types (Play X) compared to a baseline (e.g., average opponent PPP or opponent PPP after other play types), adjusted for confounding factors. Also, the average difference in opponent next-possession PPP conditional on own-sequence lags (e.g., 'Play X' preceded by 'Play A').",
  "unit": "Possession. The primary unit of analysis for measuring PPP will be the opponent's offensive possession immediately following the team's 'Play X'. For own-sequence lags, the unit will be 'Play X' preceded by a specific 'Play A'.",
  "sample": "All NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and complete 2025-26 regular season and playoff games. Filter for possessions where 'Play X' is clearly identified and the subsequent opponent possession is also identifiable. Exclude possessions with known labeling issues (e.g., OTHER/DRIVE fouls) or apply robust adjustments.",
  "power": "Estimate required sample size based on expected effect sizes from FAM:SF07 (e.g., aftershock of +0.1 to +0.15 PPP) and desired statistical power (e.g., 0.8) for detecting meaningful differences between play types. Conduct a power analysis, accounting for within-team and within-game correlations, and the frequency of less common play types.",
  "controls": "Statistical controls for confounding variables using regression models. Covariates will include: game state (score differential, time remaining in period/game), team and opponent quality metrics (e.g., overall offensive/defensive ratings), player personnel on court (starters/bench, specific lineups), transition vs. half-court possessions, and previous play outcome (e.g., turnover, made shot, missed shot). Sensitivity analysis will be performed by including/excluding possessions affected by known labeling issues (OTHER/DRIVE foul leakage) or by using adjusted PPP metrics if available.",
  "falsification": "If the observed differences in opponent next-possession PPP across various 'Play X' types are not statistically significant or fall within the expected noise, or if the effects are entirely explained by confounding factors, the hypothesis of 'AVOID labels' being driven by these effects will be falsified. Additionally, if corrections for known labeling artifacts eliminate or reverse apparent effects, the findings would be considered artifacts.",
  "multiplicity": "Apply Bonferroni correction or False Discovery Rate (FDR) control if testing multiple comparisons between numerous play types or lag sequences. Focus on a pre-specified, limited set of high-interest play types for primary analysis to manage multiplicity.",
  "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if there are statistically significant and meaningfully large differences (e.g., >0.05 PPP) in opponent next-possession PPP between offensive play types, after controlling for confounders and addressing labeling issues, and if these differences are consistent with a reasonable threshold for 'AVOID' labels. The 'own-sequence lags' component will be supported if similar significant and meaningful effects are observed.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 (complete as of 2026-06-21) regular season and playoffs play-by-play data, substitution data, box-score summaries, and derived play-type possession records from the graph database."
 }

